## The following text has been annotated for parts of speech by students at NTU Singapore as part of a class project in AY 2025.
## Verses are extracted from the taggedPBC. Annotations have been developed with a combination of manual and automated approaches, with reference to existing literature and (where possible) native speakers.
## ISO 639-3: zsm, Language: Standard Malay, Number of verses: 100
## References: Alfred, R., Mujat, A., & Obit, J.H. (2013). A Rule-Based Part of Speech (RPOS) Tagger for Malay Text Articles. In H. Haron, A. Selamat, & N. Nguyen (Eds.), INTELLIGENT INFORMATION AND DATABASE SYSTEMS (ACIIDS 2013), PT II (Vol. 7803, pp. 50-59). Springer Nature ; Eberhard, David M., Gary F. Simons, and Charles D. Fennig (eds.). 2025. Ethnologue: Languages of the World. Twenty-eighth edition. Dallas, Texas: SIL International. Online version: https://www.ethnologue.com/language/zsm/ ; Kiue, C. Y. (2024, March 25). Richness of the malay language: 7 captivating facts revealed about bahasa melayu. https://languageeducatorsassemble.com/malay-language/ ; Omar, A. (1989). The Malay spelling reform. Journal of the Simplified Spelling Society. https://web.archive.org/web/20110826053108/http://www.englishspellingsociety.org/journals/j11/malay.php ; Wichmann, Søren, Eric W. Holman, Cecil H. Brown, Matthew S. Dryer, and Qibin Ran (eds.). 2025. The ASJP Database (version 21).
